{#- TODO-SARAH: the testimonials themselves. All three are the design's
    placeholder, which announces itself on the page. Sarah's real words replace
    the two strings per entry and nothing else.

    A list plus a loop rather than three copy-pasted blocks, the same call
    header.njk made for the nav: the markup is identical every time and only the
    strings differ.

    The em dash sits in the template below and only the name and role are data,
    so a replacement is a paste of Sarah's text with no punctuation to match. -#}
{% set testimonials = [
  {
    "quote": "Placeholder testimonial — three to four lines of client copy from Sarah goes here, fixed-size block.",
    "by": "Client name, role"
  },
  {
    "quote": "Placeholder testimonial — three to four lines of client copy from Sarah goes here, fixed-size block.",
    "by": "Client name, role"
  },
  {
    "quote": "Placeholder testimonial — three to four lines of client copy from Sarah goes here, fixed-size block.",
    "by": "Client name, role"
  }
] %}
<div class="page-head">
  <h1 class="page-head__title">What clients say</h1>
  <p class="page-head__lead">A few words from people Sarah has coached.</p>
</div>
<section class="testimonials">
  {%- for t in testimonials %}
    {#- A blockquote where the design has a plain div, and a footer where it has a
      second span — these are quotations with an attribution, the same call
      index.njk made for the homepage teaser. 

      aria-hidden on the quote glyph. It is ornament, and without this a screen
      reader announces a stray quote mark before every testimonial. -#}
    <blockquote class="testimonial">
      <span class="testimonial__mark" aria-hidden="true">"</span>
      <p class="testimonial__quote">{{ t.quote }}</p>
      <footer class="testimonial__by">
        — {{ t.by }}
      </footer>
    </blockquote>
  {%- endfor %}
  <a class="button" href="/book/">Book a consultation</a>
</section>
